import fs from 'node:fs';
const chapters=[];const raw=[];
function c(title,points,flow,script,questions){chapters.push({title,formula:points[0],points,board:{kind:'flow',lines:points,flow},script});raw.push(...questions)}
const q=(question,correct,wrong,explanation)=>[question,correct,wrong,explanation];
c('Biotechnology: scope and two core principles',
['Biotechnology uses organisms, cells or their products','Genetic engineering changes inherited information','Bioprocess engineering controls production conditions','A useful product still needs evidence and quality checks'],
['Biological system + defined objective','Genetic change where appropriate','Controlled production and recovery','Evidence of identity, quality and effect'],
`Welcome to Biotechnology and Its Applications, our NEET Biology Unit nine foundation. This recording uses an AI generated Marin voice. Biotechnology applies biological systems, cells or their products to useful purposes. Traditional fermentation is biotechnology even without deliberate recombinant DNA construction. Modern biotechnology combines genetic engineering with controlled bioprocessing. Genetic engineering changes genetic information or its arrangement, while bioprocess engineering creates suitable conditions for biological production and product recovery. These are related activities rather than alternative names for one enzyme. A DNA sequence, its RNA, the encoded protein and a finished medicine are different levels of description. Producing more DNA does not automatically produce a safe, effective final product. We will connect molecular tools with agriculture, health, transgenic animals and biosafety. This is a foundation recording against the dated official syllabus baseline, not a laboratory protocol or a claim of exhaustive mastery. Further labelled-vector problems, experimental controls and mixed application cases remain for subsequent practice.`,
[q('Which activity can count as biotechnology without recombinant DNA construction?','Traditional microbial fermentation',['Only DNA sequencing','Only a chromosome deletion','Only inserting an animal gene'],'Biotechnology includes older biological production processes.'),q('Controlled conditions and product recovery chiefly concern:','Bioprocess engineering',['Only genetic inheritance ratios','Only restriction recognition','Only an antibody binding site'],'Genetic design and production control are distinct tasks.')]);
c('Recombinant DNA and the first cloning logic',
['Recombinant DNA joins DNA in a new arrangement','A vector carries an inserted DNA segment','A host can copy the recombinant molecule','Cloning DNA differs from cloning a whole organism'],
['DNA of interest + suitable carrier','Recombinant molecule','Compatible host maintains copies','Verify the intended construct'],
`Recombinant DNA contains DNA segments brought together in a new arrangement. The participating segments need not always come from different species. A cloning vector is a carrier that can maintain an inserted sequence in an appropriate host. In the historical Cohen and Boyer comparison, plasmid based recombinant DNA could be introduced into bacteria and propagated, helping establish molecular cloning. Copying an inserted DNA sequence is different from producing a genetically identical whole animal. It is also different from expressing a protein, because a copied sequence may lack the regulatory context needed for expression. Consider an original DNA fragment and a carrier as separate components before joining them conceptually. The host supplies cellular machinery that can replicate compatible DNA, but successful entry alone does not prove the desired insert is present. Sequence identity, arrangement and relevant function require verification. This distinction explains why later stages include selection and screening rather than assuming every cell contains the same successful recombinant molecule.`,
[q('Recombinant DNA must always combine DNA from different species. This statement is:','Too restrictive; new arrangements can involve the same species',['Always correct','The definition of a host','The definition of a protein'],'Species origin alone does not define every recombinant arrangement.'),q('Molecular cloning chiefly copies:','A selected DNA sequence',['An entire animal in every case','Only an antibody molecule','Only mature insulin protein'],'DNA cloning and organism cloning differ.')]);
c('Restriction enzymes and recognition specificity',
['Restriction endonucleases cut within DNA','Recognition sequences determine many cut locations','Exonucleases remove nucleotides from DNA ends','Restriction and ligation perform different jobs'],
['Double-stranded DNA substrate','Recognition of a suitable site','Cleavage within the DNA molecule','Defined fragments and ends'],
`Nucleases act on nucleic acids, but their positions of action differ. An exonuclease removes nucleotides from an end, whereas an endonuclease cleaves within a nucleic acid molecule. Restriction endonucleases used in basic recombinant DNA examples recognize particular sequences and cut at defined positions in relation to those sequences. Their specificity makes DNA fragmentation more predictable than random breakage. Bacterial restriction and modification systems also illustrate a natural defense relationship involving recognition and protection of DNA. Do not assume every nuclease recognizes the same sequence or produces the same type of end. The name of an enzyme, the recognition site and the resulting fragments answer three different questions. Cutting a vector does not join an insert, and cutting DNA does not by itself copy it. For examination reasoning, first decide whether the required action is internal cleavage, end removal, synthesis or joining. Then identify the tool whose activity actually fits that action in the stated molecular setting.`,
[q('Which action characterizes an endonuclease?','Cleavage within a nucleic acid molecule',['Only removing terminal nucleotides','Joining all DNA ends','Translation of messenger RNA'],'Position of cleavage distinguishes endonucleases from exonucleases.'),q('Restriction-enzyme specificity primarily concerns:','Recognition of particular DNA sequences',['Recognition of every protein hormone','Only a host body size','Only the color of a colony'],'Different tools recognize different molecular substrates.')]);
c('EcoRI: palindrome, polarity and sticky ends',
['EcoRI recognizes the familiar GAATTC site','Read both strands in the same 5′ to 3′ direction','Staggered cleavage can leave cohesive ends','Compatible ends still require covalent joining'],
['5′ G A A T T C 3′','3′ C T T A A G 5′','EcoRI cuts: G | AATTC','AATT overhangs can pair'],
`EcoRI provides a familiar example of a restriction endonuclease recognizing a palindromic DNA sequence. Write one strand from five prime to three prime as G A A T T C. Its complementary strand, written alongside it, runs three prime to five prime as C T T A A G. Reading that second strand in its own five prime to three prime direction again gives G A A T T C. That directional relationship defines the palindrome here; it is not simply reading one printed strand backward without considering complementarity. EcoRI cleaves between the G and the adjacent A in the standard site, leaving cohesive A A T T overhangs. Complementary overhangs can base pair, helping align compatible fragments. Base pairing alone is not a complete covalent seal of the sugar phosphate backbone. DNA ligase supplies the joining activity discussed next. Always label strand polarity before deciding whether a proposed sequence is palindromic or whether two ends are compatible.`,
[q('The familiar EcoRI recognition sequence, written 5′ to 3′, is:','GAATTC',['GGGGGG','ATATAG','AAAAAC'],'Its complementary strand gives the same sequence when read in its own 5′ to 3′ direction.'),q('Cohesive-end base pairing by itself provides:','Alignment rather than a completed covalent backbone seal',['A newly translated insulin molecule','Unlimited DNA replication','The final purification of a vaccine'],'Ligase and complementary base pairing have different roles.')]);
c('Ligase, polymerase and reverse transcriptase',
['Ligase forms covalent links in DNA backbones','DNA polymerase synthesizes DNA from a template','Reverse transcriptase makes DNA using RNA','An intron-free cDNA differs from genomic DNA'],
['Joining: DNA ligase','DNA copying: DNA polymerase','RNA template → DNA: reverse transcriptase','Match the enzyme to its substrate and action'],
`DNA ligase joins appropriately positioned DNA ends through covalent backbone links. DNA polymerase instead extends a DNA chain using a template and a suitable starting end. These activities should not be exchanged merely because both enzymes appear in recombinant DNA work. Reverse transcriptase uses RNA as a template to make DNA, reversing the usual transcription direction in the elementary comparison. Complementary DNA made from a processed eukaryotic messenger RNA represents the processed transcript and therefore lacks introns removed during RNA processing. The corresponding genomic region can contain those introns as well as other regulatory information. This matters when comparing a eukaryotic coding sequence with expression in bacteria, whose machinery does not ordinarily remove eukaryotic spliceosomal introns. An intron free sequence still needs appropriate expression signals; its presence alone does not guarantee protein production. Ask separately what molecule is copied, which direction information flows, which enzyme acts and whether the resulting DNA can be expressed in the particular host.`,
[q('Which enzyme uses RNA as a template to produce DNA?','Reverse transcriptase',['DNA ligase','A restriction endonuclease','A protease'],'The template is RNA and the new molecule is DNA.'),q('cDNA from processed eukaryotic mRNA usually lacks:','Introns removed during RNA processing',['Every coding nucleotide','All complementarity','Every possible regulatory requirement'],'A processed transcript differs from its genomic locus.')]);
c('Vector essentials: origin, marker and cloning site',
['An origin supports replication in a suitable host','A selectable marker helps distinguish transformants','A cloning site provides an insertion location','Expression requires appropriate regulatory signals'],
['Origin of replication','Selectable marker','Suitable insertion site','Host-compatible maintenance / expression'],
`A useful cloning vector has features serving different functions. Its origin of replication allows replication to begin in a suitable host and can influence copy number. A selectable marker helps distinguish cells that acquired the relevant selectable trait from those that did not. A suitable cloning site provides a defined region where DNA can be inserted without destroying every essential vector function. These labels should not be treated as interchangeable parts of a circular diagram. An origin is not an antibiotic resistance protein, and a selectable marker is not automatically the gene whose product is desired. Cloning vectors emphasize maintenance and copying, whereas expression constructs also require regulatory elements appropriate for producing RNA and protein in the chosen system. A host compatible origin and an intact selectable marker do not establish that the intended insert is present or correctly arranged. That is why selection, insert screening and expression testing answer related but distinct questions about the outcome of a genetic manipulation.`,
[q('Which vector feature chiefly supports initiation of replication?','Origin of replication',['Only an antigen-binding site','Only mature C-peptide','Only a host receptor'],'The origin and selectable marker serve different purposes.'),q('Selection of vector-bearing cells proves that the desired insert is correct. This is:','Incorrect; insert screening and verification remain necessary',['Always correct','The definition of replication','The definition of ligation'],'A selectable trait is narrower evidence than complete construct identity.')]);
c('pBR322 and insertional inactivation',
['pBR322 is a familiar bacterial plasmid vector','ampR and tetR are different selectable markers','An insert may disrupt an existing marker','Interpret survival using the stated insertion site'],
['Schematic pBR322: ori + ampR + tetR','Insert disrupts tetR in the example','ampicillin resistant / tetracycline sensitive','Marker disruption helps identify recombinants'],
`The familiar pBR322 plasmid diagram includes an origin and ampicillin and tetracycline resistance markers, usually written amp R and tet R. The diagram represents a vector rather than a bacterial chromosome or an animal organ. In a standard insertional inactivation example, foreign DNA interrupts the tetracycline resistance region while the ampicillin resistance function remains intact. Such a recombinant can retain ampicillin resistance but lose tetracycline resistance under the stated assumptions. A cell carrying an intact nonrecombinant vector can retain both traits. Therefore ampicillin resistance alone does not distinguish those two vector bearing possibilities. The inference depends on which region was disrupted; it should not be memorized as a universal property of every insert at every site. Also do not assign beta galactosidase blue white screening to unmodified pBR322 as though it were the same marker system. Read the vector map and the specified insertion before predicting the selectable phenotype in a comparison question.`,
[q('If insertion disrupts tetR but preserves ampR, the expected recombinant phenotype is:','Ampicillin resistant and tetracycline sensitive',['Sensitive to both by definition','Resistant to tetracycline only','Blue in every possible screening system'],'The inference depends on the disrupted marker.'),q('Unmodified pBR322 and lacZ blue-white screening are:','Different marker-system examples',['Identical definitions','Both only insulin processing','Both only RNA interference'],'Do not transfer one vector system’s marker logic to another.')]);
c('Selection versus screening: blue and white logic',
['Selection asks which cells show a chosen trait','Screening distinguishes relevant survivors','lacZ disruption can alter reporter activity','Reporter phenotype is evidence, not full sequence proof'],
['Vector-bearing candidates','Reporter intact or disrupted','Color difference under stated assay conditions','Confirm the intended insert separately'],
`Selection and screening solve different identification problems. Selection favors cells with a particular trait, such as the ability to survive the stated selective condition. Screening then helps distinguish candidates within that surviving group. In the familiar lac Z alpha blue white example, an intact reporter system can yield blue colonies on a suitable chromogenic substrate, whereas an insert disrupting the relevant reporter region can give white colonies. This interpretation assumes an appropriate host, vector and assay context. White appearance does not universally mean that every desired DNA sequence is present in exactly the right orientation. Other events can also disrupt reporter activity. Likewise, absence of a colony can reflect more than one failed step. Follow the evidence in stages: did the cell acquire a selectable trait, was a reporter disrupted, and was the actual insert verified? Keeping those questions separate prevents a common examination error in which one visible result is treated as proof of every molecular event.`,
[q('In the stated lacZ insertional-inactivation system, a disrupted reporter commonly gives:','White candidate colonies',['Blue colonies in every case','Only an antibody precipitate','Only methane'],'The result depends on the host, vector and assay context.'),q('A white candidate colony establishes the full insert sequence without confirmation. This is:','Incorrect; other causes of reporter disruption exist',['Always correct','The definition of a plasmid','The definition of a vaccine'],'Screening narrows candidates but is not full sequence verification.')]);
c('Hosts and DNA delivery: biological compatibility',
['A host supports maintenance or expression','Transformation and expression are distinct outcomes','Disarmed vectors repurpose natural transfer systems','Plant and animal delivery systems differ'],
['Suitable host and construct','DNA entry by an appropriate system','Maintenance / expression where supported','Verify identity and biological outcome'],
`A host is the cellular system in which introduced material may be maintained or expressed. DNA entry does not automatically guarantee stable inheritance, expression or the desired phenotype. Bacterial transformation, plant genetic transfer and animal cell delivery involve different biological contexts. In the textbook plant comparison, a disarmed tumor inducing plasmid system from Agrobacterium is repurposed so that useful transfer functions can be considered separately from disease causing effects. Agrobacterium tumefaciens is the familiar textbook name. Modified viral vectors provide another conceptual delivery comparison in appropriate animal systems. Physical delivery approaches also exist, but the key foundation idea is matching a carrier and host rather than memorizing one universal method. A bacterial promoter may not function appropriately in a plant cell, and a plant host does not become a bacterium because it contains an introduced sequence. For any example, distinguish carrier identity, recipient cell, genetic maintenance, expression and the evidence needed to demonstrate the intended biological result.`,
[q('A disarmed Ti-plasmid system is associated with the familiar textbook plant-transfer example involving:','Agrobacterium tumefaciens',['Only Plasmodium vivax','Only human haemoglobin','Only a methanogen'],'Natural transfer functions can be repurposed separately from tumor-related effects.'),q('DNA entry into a cell necessarily guarantees stable expression. This is:','Incorrect; maintenance and expression require additional conditions',['Always correct','The definition of a host','The definition of transcription'],'Entry, inheritance and expression are separate outcomes.')]);
c('DNA isolation and electrophoresis interpretation',
['Cell material contains DNA plus many other components','DNA separation differs from sequence identification','DNA migrates toward the positive electrode','Smaller linear fragments usually move farther in agarose'],
['DNA mixture placed near negative end','Electric field across an agarose matrix','Fragments separate by migration behavior','Compare bands with a size reference'],
`DNA isolation separates genetic material from a mixture containing membranes, proteins, RNA and other cellular components. It should not be confused with reading a DNA sequence. Agarose gel electrophoresis then separates DNA fragments according to their migration under an electric field. DNA has a negatively charged phosphate backbone and moves toward the positive electrode. In a standard comparison of linear double stranded fragments under the same gel conditions, smaller fragments usually migrate farther than larger ones. Compare a sample band with an appropriate size reference rather than assuming the brightest band is necessarily the largest fragment. Band intensity can reflect DNA amount as well as staining and measurement conditions. Circular plasmid topology can alter migration, so the simple size rule should not be applied carelessly to every molecular form. A band of approximately the expected size supports a size comparison but does not alone establish sequence identity, purity or correct expression of the DNA it contains.`,
[q('DNA migrates in a standard gel toward the:','Positive electrode',['Negative electrode because DNA is positive','Largest protein in the room','Origin of replication only'],'Its phosphate backbone gives DNA a net negative charge.'),q('For comparable linear DNA fragments, farther migration usually indicates:','A smaller fragment under the same gel conditions',['A guaranteed larger fragment','A higher protein expression level','A fully verified nucleotide sequence'],'Topology and experimental context limit the simple size rule.')]);
c('PCR: the three-stage amplification cycle',
['PCR amplifies a defined DNA region','Denaturation separates template strands','Primers anneal to complementary regions','Thermostable polymerase extends new DNA'],
['Denaturation: template strands separate','Annealing: primers bind','Extension: DNA polymerase synthesizes','Repeated cycles enrich the target region'],
`Polymerase chain reaction, or PCR, amplifies a selected DNA region using repeated cycles of strand separation, primer binding and DNA synthesis. During denaturation, complementary template strands separate. During annealing, short primers bind to suitable complementary sequences. During extension, a DNA polymerase synthesizes new DNA from the available starting ends. A thermostable polymerase, with Taq as the familiar textbook example, tolerates the repeated heating involved in the basic process. Primers help define which region is amplified; they are not enzymes and do not themselves join vector backbones. PCR does not require a living bacterial cell to copy the target in this conceptual comparison. The amplified material is DNA rather than automatically an encoded protein. Distinguish amplification from cloning in a host and from detection of a protein antigen. The three named stages describe a molecular logic that can be recognized in an examination flow diagram without treating this classroom account as an experimental operating procedure.`,
[q('The standard PCR stage order is:','Denaturation, annealing, extension',['Translation, ligation, digestion','Annealing, translation, secretion','Purification, vaccination, fertilization'],'Template separation precedes primer binding and synthesis.'),q('Primers in PCR are:','Short nucleic acid sequences that help define the target',['Enzymes that ligate every plasmid','Complete bacterial cells','Preformed insulin proteins'],'Primers and polymerase perform different roles.')]);
c('PCR numbers, reverse transcription and controls',
['Ideal amplification follows N = N₀ × 2ⁿ','Real reactions need not double perfectly','RNA targets require a DNA-conversion step','Controls distinguish evidence from contamination'],
['One starting target → ideal doubling','Three ideal cycles → eight copies','RNA → cDNA before appropriate PCR','Interpret positive and negative controls'],
`In the idealized model, each amplification cycle doubles the number of target copies. Starting with one target copy, three perfect cycles produce eight copies, while ten perfect cycles produce one thousand and twenty four. The general relation is the starting number multiplied by two raised to the number of cycles. Real reactions can depart from this ideal because efficiency changes and limiting conditions develop. This arithmetic is a model rather than a guarantee about every sample. Standard PCR copies DNA, so an RNA target is first represented as DNA by reverse transcription in the relevant assay. Reverse transcription PCR and real time quantitative PCR describe different features, even though some assays combine them. A negative control helps reveal contamination, and appropriate positive and internal controls help interpret a negative sample result. Neither a positive signal nor a negative signal should be considered independently of sampling, specificity and controls. Ask exactly which molecule the assay detects.`,
[q('Starting with two copies, five ideal doubling cycles yield:','64 copies',['10 copies','32 copies','128 copies'],'Two multiplied by 2⁵ equals 64.'),q('An RNA target is represented for conventional PCR amplification using:','Reverse transcription to make cDNA',['Only DNA ligase to make protein','Only a chromogenic antibody','Only gel staining'],'PCR amplifies DNA; reverse transcription supplies DNA from RNA.')]);
c('From a recombinant construct to a verified product',
['Construction, copying and expression are different','Expression links DNA to RNA and protein','An observed trait requires relevant evidence','Verification belongs throughout the workflow'],
['Construct and identify recombinant DNA','Maintain it in a suitable system','Assess expression and product identity','Recover product and evaluate quality'],
`A recombinant workflow can be understood as a sequence of questions rather than a list of interchangeable enzymes. What DNA arrangement is intended? Can the chosen system maintain it? Is the relevant RNA or protein produced? Does the recovered material have the expected identity and activity? Restriction, ligation, introduction, selection, screening, expression and recovery address different parts of that reasoning. A construct containing a coding region is not equivalent to a finished medicine. A detectable transcript is not proof that a correctly folded protein was produced. A band of expected size is not proof of biological activity. Controls and independent measurements narrow these uncertainties at appropriate stages. Consider a hypothetical harmless enzyme product: copying its gene establishes DNA availability, measuring its protein addresses expression, and measuring its catalytic behavior addresses function. These observations support different conclusions. The same evidence discipline applies when interpreting crop traits, diagnostic assays and transgenic animal experiments in later chapters of this foundation recording.`,
[q('Detecting a transcript directly establishes:','Evidence of RNA expression, not every later protein property',['Guaranteed clinical effectiveness','Guaranteed protein folding and purity','Automatic patent eligibility'],'RNA, protein identity and function are different measurement levels.'),q('Which observation most directly concerns an enzyme product’s function?','Measurement of its catalytic activity',['Only a vector-bearing colony','Only an origin label','Only a DNA size estimate'],'The relevant measurement should match the proposed conclusion.')]);
c('Bioreactors and downstream processing',
['Bioreactors provide controlled production environments','Mixing and exchange depend on the biological system','Downstream processing recovers and purifies product','Quality assessment extends beyond product quantity'],
['Suitable biological producer','Controlled bioreactor environment','Recovery and purification','Identity, activity and quality assessment'],
`A bioreactor provides an organized environment for a biological production process. Stirred tank designs are familiar textbook examples in which mixing, temperature, acidity and exchange with the surroundings can be managed according to the organism and product. Aeration is important for appropriate aerobic systems, but oxygen supply is not identical for every biological process. More stirring or more oxygen is not universally better for every cell or molecule. Upstream production and downstream processing answer different needs. Downstream work includes recovery, separation and purification, followed by relevant formulation and quality assessment for the intended product. A high amount of crude material does not establish that the active product is pure, correctly identified or suitable for use. For examination diagrams, associate the impeller with mixing and distinguish production vessels from later recovery stages. The foundation goal is understanding the roles of controlled growth and product processing, rather than treating one vessel design as a complete universal recipe for all biotechnology.`,
[q('Downstream processing chiefly concerns:','Recovery, separation and purification after production',['Only recognition of EcoRI sites','Only DNA inheritance ratios','Only pollen germination'],'Production quantity and finished product quality differ.'),q('Aeration requirements in bioreactors are:','Dependent on the organism and process',['Identical for every anaerobic and aerobic system','Always zero','Unrelated to metabolism'],'Biological production conditions must fit the actual system.')]);
c('Agricultural applications and the trait distinction',
['GM describes genetic alteration, not one fixed trait','Traits can concern pests, stress or nutrition','A proposed benefit requires an appropriate comparison','Genotype, environment and management interact'],
['Defined agricultural objective','Specific genetic trait','Measured outcome against a comparator','Environmental and food-safety assessment'],
`Agricultural biotechnology can address pest damage, stress responses, nutritional composition and other production objectives. Genetically modified describes a type of genetic alteration rather than one universal beneficial trait. A crop engineered for a particular insect target is not thereby resistant to every insect, every disease or every drought. A nutritional change does not automatically increase yield in every environment. Ask which gene or change is involved, which biological trait is expected and how that outcome was measured against an appropriate comparison. Tissue culture and conventional breeding also contribute to agriculture, but they are not automatically the same as introducing a recombinant construct. A plant's performance depends on its genetic background, environmental conditions and management. Therefore a useful question states the actual trait and context before comparing benefits or risks. In the next chapters, Bt proteins and RNA interference illustrate two different molecular mechanisms for pest related effects, rather than two names for the same engineered plant characteristic.`,
[q('A crop engineered against one pest is necessarily resistant to every disease. This is:','Incorrect; the trait has a defined biological scope',['Always correct','The definition of a transgene','The definition of nutrition'],'One trait does not establish unrelated protections.'),q('The strongest assessment of an agricultural claim includes:','A relevant comparator and measured outcome',['Only the label GM','Only the length of the crop name','Only an assumed universal benefit'],'Evidence must address the particular proposed effect.')]);
c('Bt: source, protoxin and susceptible insect gut',
['Bacillus thuringiensis supplies familiar cry genes','Cry proteins differ from the genes encoding them','Activation and target binding affect susceptibility','The effect is not universal across all organisms'],
['Bt gene → insect-active protein','Ingestion by a susceptible insect','Gut conditions activate the familiar protoxin','Target binding and membrane damage'],
`Bacillus thuringiensis, abbreviated Bt, is a bacterium associated with insect active proteins. In the standard textbook account, protein crystals contain an inactive protoxin form that becomes active in the alkaline gut environment of a susceptible insect. Activation, binding to appropriate gut cell targets and membrane disruption contribute to the damaging effect. The bacterium, its cry gene and the Cry protein are separate entities. A plant expressing a relevant introduced gene makes the corresponding protein; it does not become a bacterial cell. Susceptibility depends on the particular protein and insect biology, so the mechanism should not be described as killing every organism indiscriminately. The simple classroom mechanism explains the familiar comparison, while real protein variants and crop events require their own characterization. When reading a statement about Bt, identify whether it names the source organism, the encoded product, a susceptible insect or the engineered plant. This role separation prevents confusing microbial biocontrol with plant expression of an insect active protein.`,
[q('The source organism in the familiar Bt comparison is:','Bacillus thuringiensis',['Meloidogyne incognita','Saccharomyces cerevisiae','Agrobacterium as the definition of Bt'],'Bt is a bacterium, while Cry proteins are its product examples.'),q('The textbook protoxin is activated in the familiar susceptible insect comparison by:','Conditions in the insect gut',['Human insulin processing','Every plant nucleus automatically','Antibody memory alone'],'Activation and target susceptibility are central to the mechanism.')]);
c('Cry gene examples and resistance reasoning',
['cry1Ac and cry2Ab are cotton bollworm examples','cry1Ab is a familiar corn-borer comparison','Different Cry proteins have different target ranges','Resistance can evolve under selection'],
['Specified cry gene / protein','Specified susceptible target','Measure pest effect and crop performance','Monitor resistance and non-target outcomes'],
`The textbook examples associate cry one A c and cry two A b with cotton bollworm control, while cry one A b is a familiar corn borer comparison. Older print conventions may use Roman numeral forms in these names. Treat the names as particular gene and protein examples rather than a universal code stating that every Cry protein affects every insect equally. The crop, the protein and the susceptible pest must be matched in the actual question. An introduced trait can reduce damage by a relevant target without eliminating every agricultural problem. Pest populations also contain heritable variation, and selection can increase variants with reduced susceptibility. Resistance management and monitoring therefore remain important even after an effective trait is introduced. A resistance outcome does not imply that an individual insect intentionally learned to redesign its genome. Connect population level selection with the molecular specificity discussed in the previous chapter, and distinguish short term efficacy from durable performance over many generations.`,
[q('The familiar corn-borer gene comparison is:','cry1Ab',['ADA only','lacZ only','Human alpha-lactalbumin only'],'The named Cry examples have different textbook target associations.'),q('Reduced susceptibility can spread in a pest population through:','Selection on heritable variation',['Insects intentionally choosing mutations','Every Cry gene becoming an antibody','Plants turning into mosquitoes'],'Population evolution differs from purposeful individual adaptation.')]);
c('RNA interference and the tobacco nematode example',
['RNA interference can reduce target RNA activity','Sequence complementarity contributes specificity','Meloidogyne incognita is the nematode example','RNA silencing differs from Bt protein action'],
['Complementary RNA relationships','Double-stranded RNA-related silencing pathway','Reduced expression of a matching target','Nematode survival / parasitism can be affected'],
`RNA interference, often called RNA i, is a gene silencing process involving RNA and sequence directed effects on target RNA. Double stranded RNA related pathways can guide reduced expression through matching messenger RNA rather than by cutting every genomic DNA sequence indiscriminately. The standard agricultural example concerns Meloidogyne incognita, a root knot nematode that infects tobacco roots. The textbook comparison describes complementary RNA production in the engineered plant and silencing of a relevant nematode target, interfering with successful parasitism. This is a conceptual explanation of sequence dependent biological interference, not an experimental design or a delivery protocol. Bt protection instead emphasizes an insect active protein acting on susceptible gut cells. RNA interference should therefore not be called another name for a Cry protein or for antibiotic treatment. Track which organism contains the engineered change, which organism supplies the target and which molecule is reduced in the stated account. Those distinctions make the nematode example easier to reason through.`,
[q('Meloidogyne incognita in the textbook RNAi example is a:','Root-knot nematode',['Cotton plant','Restriction enzyme','Hepatitis vaccine antigen'],'The pest, engineered plant and target RNA are separate roles.'),q('RNA interference chiefly illustrates:','Sequence-directed reduction of target RNA expression',['Universal destruction of every DNA molecule','Covalent ligation of a plasmid','Insulin C-peptide removal'],'Its mechanism differs from Cry-protein action.')]);
c('Recombinant insulin: precursor and mature hormone',
['Insulin is a peptide hormone, not a DNA enzyme','Mature human insulin has A and B chains','Disulfide bonds stabilize the mature structure','C-peptide belongs to proinsulin processing'],
['Proinsulin precursor','Connecting C-peptide removed during maturation','A chain + B chain remain','Disulfide-linked mature insulin'],
`Human insulin is a peptide hormone with a mature structure containing an A chain and a B chain connected by disulfide bonds. The A chain contains twenty one amino acids and the B chain contains thirty. During normal human biosynthesis, an initial precursor is processed through proinsulin, whose connecting C peptide is removed during maturation. Mature insulin therefore does not retain the connecting C peptide as a third permanent hormone chain. The structure helps explain why expressing a precursor and obtaining a correctly processed active product are different tasks. Insulin regulates metabolism; it is not an antibody, a restriction enzyme or a DNA polymerase. The recombinant medicine comparison concerns producing an appropriately characterized protein using engineered biological systems. Do not confuse the coding DNA with the secreted hormone or assume that any protein of similar size has the same activity. Distinguish precursor, processed chains, linking bonds and product function before comparing different production strategies in the next chapter.`,
[q('Mature human insulin contains:','A and B chains linked by disulfide bonds',['A permanent A, B and C chain complex','Only C-peptide','Only one DNA strand'],'C-peptide is removed during normal proinsulin maturation.'),q('Human insulin A and B chains contain respectively:','21 and 30 amino acids',['30 and 21 amino acids','21 and 21 amino acids','30 and 30 amino acids'],'Chain identity matters when interpreting the mature structure.')]);
c('Insulin production: historical strategy and product quality',
['The historical Eli Lilly example used separate chains','Engineered bacteria supplied A and B chain products','Appropriate joining yielded the mature comparison','Modern processes need not use the identical route'],
['Separate A-chain / B-chain coding constructs','Host expression of chain products','Recovery and appropriate chain assembly','Verified mature insulin product'],
`The familiar historical recombinant insulin example describes Eli Lilly producing the A and B chain products using engineered Escherichia coli systems and then combining the chains with appropriate disulfide linkages. This separates the challenge of obtaining mature chain products from relying on bacterial cells to perform every step of normal human proinsulin processing. The example explains a landmark production strategy; it is not a claim that all modern insulin products use precisely the same manufacturing route. Other expression systems and precursor processing strategies exist. Historical animal pancreatic extraction and recombinant production also differ in source and control of product identity. Neither source label alone replaces evidence about purity, activity and quality. A bacterium carrying a human coding sequence remains a bacterium while producing a specified protein product. In an examination question, match the source organism, expression host, precursor or chain form and final hormone rather than assuming they are the same object. Keep the historical case separate from universal statements about current manufacturing practice.`,
[q('The familiar historical Eli Lilly recombinant strategy emphasized:','Separate A and B chain production followed by assembly',['Retention of C-peptide in every mature hormone','Production by a cotton bollworm','Antibody-only detection with no protein production'],'The historical account does not define every modern manufacturing route.'),q('A bacterium expressing an insulin coding sequence becomes a human cell. This is:','Incorrect; expression of a protein does not change its species identity',['Always correct','The definition of recombinant insulin','The definition of a precursor'],'Host identity and expressed product identity differ.')]);
c('Recombinant vaccines and the hepatitis B example',
['A vaccine antigen can be produced recombinantly','HBsAg is a hepatitis B surface-antigen example','Yeast can serve as an antigen-expression host','Vaccination aims to induce an active immune response'],
['Selected antigen coding information','Expression in an appropriate producer','Purified antigen in a defined formulation','Recipient mounts a specific immune response'],
`Recombinant vaccine production uses engineered biological systems to obtain a defined antigen or another specified vaccine component. Hepatitis B surface antigen, commonly abbreviated H B s A g, is a familiar example produced in suitable yeast systems. The antigen is recovered and incorporated into a characterized vaccine formulation. The producer yeast and the viral surface antigen are not the same entity, and the antigen does not represent a complete infectious hepatitis B virus. The immune system responds to the antigen and can develop protective immune memory in an active response. This differs from passive transfer of preformed antibodies. Recombinant is a production description rather than a guarantee that every vaccine uses the same platform, host or antigen format. A useful comparison identifies the antigen, the producer and the recipient response separately. Product quality and clinical evidence remain necessary alongside the molecular concept. This classroom account explains biological principles and does not prescribe an individual vaccination schedule or replace product specific medical guidance.`,
[q('HBsAg refers to:','Hepatitis B surface antigen',['A complete producer yeast chromosome','Human insulin C-peptide','An ampicillin resistance marker'],'The antigen and its expression host have different identities.'),q('A successful vaccine-induced recipient response is chiefly:','Active immunity rather than passive antibody transfer',['Passive transfer by definition in every vaccine','Only PCR amplification','Only a restriction digest'],'The recipient mounts the relevant immune response.')]);
c('Gene therapy: somatic change and delivery concepts',
['Gene therapy changes or supplies genetic function','Somatic treatment targets the treated person’s cells','Ex vivo and in vivo describe delivery contexts','A treated somatic cell is not a germline change'],
['Defined genetic or functional problem','Genetic intervention in target cells','Assess restored or altered function','Evaluate durability, safety and clinical outcome'],
`Gene therapy aims to alter genetic function in relevant cells, for example by providing a functional gene copy or by another genetic intervention. Supplying a gene is different from administering only the missing protein. Somatic treatment addresses cells of the treated person and does not automatically create an inherited germline change in future children. In an ex vivo approach, cells are handled outside the body before being returned, whereas in vivo delivery acts within the body. These terms describe delivery context rather than two names for one particular enzyme. The appropriate strategy depends on the condition, cell type and intervention. Useful evidence includes target cell function, persistence of the effect and relevant safety outcomes. A changed DNA measurement alone does not establish a permanent clinical cure. The basic distinction prepares us for the historical adenosine deaminase example next. Keep a therapeutic goal, a biological mechanism and an observed patient outcome separate, and avoid treating every genetic disease as though it required one identical intervention.`,
[q('Somatic gene therapy automatically changes the future offspring’s inherited genome. This is:','Incorrect; somatic and germline changes differ',['Always correct','The definition of ex vivo','The definition of a vector'],'Treating somatic cells does not by itself modify the germline.'),q('Ex vivo refers to an approach in which relevant cells are handled:','Outside the body before return',['Only inside the plant chloroplast','Only in the insect gut','Only as purified antibodies'],'It describes the intervention context.')]);
c('ADA deficiency and the historical gene-therapy case',
['ADA means adenosine deaminase','ADA deficiency can cause a form of SCID','The 1990 case used modified lymphocytes','Long-term benefit depends on cells and intervention'],
['ADA functional deficiency','Historical gene transfer to lymphocytes','Returned cells supply functional activity','Finite cell persistence can require repeat treatment'],
`Adenosine deaminase, abbreviated ADA, is an enzyme whose deficiency can cause a form of severe combined immunodeficiency, or SCID. Not every form of SCID is caused by ADA deficiency; several genetic mechanisms can impair immune function. The landmark nineteen ninety gene therapy account involved introducing functional ADA information into a child's lymphocytes and returning treated cells. Because such lymphocytes do not necessarily persist indefinitely, the historical strategy could require repeated treatment. This differs from merely injecting an enzyme protein, although enzyme replacement is another treatment category in the broader comparison. Approaches involving appropriately modified blood forming stem cells have a different persistence logic, but durable benefit and safety still require evidence. An older textbook suggestion about early developmental correction should not be presented as a guaranteed routine clinical cure. For the foundation question, identify the deficient enzyme, the immune consequence, the recipient cell type and why the historical cell strategy had limitations. Distinguish this teaching case from all current therapies.`,
[q('ADA deficiency can cause a form of:','Severe combined immunodeficiency',['Only cotton bollworm resistance','Only adult-onset malaria','Only blue-white screening'],'SCID has multiple genetic causes; ADA is one example.'),q('Why could the historical lymphocyte strategy require repetition?','The modified lymphocytes need not persist indefinitely',['ADA is itself a pesticide','C-peptide prevents DNA replication','All somatic changes enter the germline'],'The persistence of the treated cells affects durability.')]);
c('Molecular diagnosis: target, timing and evidence',
['Diagnosis can detect nucleic acids or proteins','PCR amplification can reveal a low-copy target','RNA-associated assays include reverse transcription','A signal requires controls and clinical context'],
['Biological question and sample','Specified molecular target','Assay with appropriate controls','Interpret the result within its limits'],
`Molecular diagnosis asks whether a specified biological target is present or altered. Amplification based assays can detect small amounts of a matching nucleic acid, helping investigate infection or genetic variation before some older detection approaches become informative. A DNA target and an RNA target require different initial molecular handling in the basic comparison. For an RNA viral target, reverse transcription supplies a DNA representation for the relevant PCR step. A probe instead uses complementary binding to recognize a matching nucleic acid sequence; it is not automatically a polymerase or an antibody. A negative test can reflect absence of the target, insufficient sample material, timing or an assay problem. A positive result also requires specificity and contamination controls. Therefore the biological question, sample quality and test design matter alongside analytical sensitivity. For examination reasoning, identify what molecule is measured and what conclusion that measurement actually supports, rather than treating every biotechnology assay as a direct measurement of disease severity or guaranteed infectiousness.`,
[q('A nucleic-acid probe recognizes its target chiefly through:','Complementary sequence binding',['Protein translation by the probe','Universal cell killing','Insulin disulfide assembly'],'Probe hybridization differs from enzyme-mediated amplification.'),q('A negative molecular result should be interpreted with:','Sampling, timing and assay controls',['No context under any circumstances','Only the sample tube color','Only the name of the producer yeast'],'Absence of signal has multiple possible explanations.')]);
c('ELISA and antigen–antibody specificity',
['ELISA uses specific antigen–antibody binding','An enzyme-linked signal supports detection','Assays may detect antigen or antibody','Signal interpretation requires suitable controls'],
['Specified antigen or antibody target','Specific binding interaction','Enzyme-associated detectable signal','Compare with controls and assay criteria'],
`An enzyme linked immunosorbent assay, abbreviated ELISA, uses antigen antibody recognition together with an enzyme associated signal. The exact arrangement determines whether the assay detects an antigen, an antibody or another specified binding target. A color or other measured signal is therefore meaningful only within the defined assay design. Do not assume every ELISA directly detects a whole infectious organism or measures its genome. PCR emphasizes nucleic acid amplification, whereas ELISA emphasizes binding recognition and an associated signal. The two methods can answer different biological questions even when they are used in investigations of the same disease. Appropriate controls and interpretation criteria distinguish a valid signal from background or nonspecific effects. An antibody response can also depend on timing, so absence of detectable antibody is not a universal proof of absence of every relevant exposure. Match the molecular target with the assay mechanism before choosing an examination answer, and state only the conclusion supported by that particular measurement.`,
[q('The central recognition interaction in ELISA is:','Antigen–antibody binding',['Restriction-site cleavage','Primer extension only','C-peptide excision'],'An enzyme-associated signal reports the specified binding event.'),q('Every ELISA directly measures the pathogen genome. This is:','Incorrect; the defined assay may detect antigen or antibody',['Always correct','The definition of reverse transcription','The definition of a DNA vector'],'Assay targets differ from nucleic acid amplification targets.')]);
c('Transgenic animals: gene function and disease models',
['A transgenic animal carries introduced genetic material','Expression can reveal gene-regulation relationships','Models can investigate disease mechanisms','An animal result does not guarantee a human outcome'],
['Defined genetic alteration','Study expression or biological phenotype','Compare with appropriate control animals','Assess relevance and limitations of the model'],
`Transgenic animals contain introduced genetic material in the intended genetic system. The word identifies a genetic modification rather than a new taxonomic group or one universal disease. Animal models can help investigate gene regulation, normal development and mechanisms involved in disease. A change in a defined gene can be compared with appropriate control animals to connect molecular function with a phenotype. However, genetic background, expression level and species differences influence the outcome. A phenotype in a mouse is evidence about that model and does not automatically establish the same result in a human patient. Transgenic mice are familiar research examples, but memorizing a precise historical percentage of all transgenic animals is less useful than understanding the biological purpose. The study design should state the changed genetic feature, the measured outcome and the comparison group. Research ethics and animal welfare also remain relevant when considering whether a proposed model and its scientific question are appropriately justified in practice.`,
[q('A transgenic disease model is chiefly useful for:','Investigating a defined mechanism under a controlled comparison',['Guaranteeing every human treatment outcome','Making all species genetically identical','Replacing every experimental control'],'Model evidence has a defined scope and limitations.'),q('An observed mouse phenotype automatically proves the same human outcome. This is:','Incorrect; model relevance needs further evidence',['Always correct','The definition of transgenic','The definition of gene regulation'],'Species and genetic-context differences can matter.')]);
c('Biological products, Rosie and safety-testing models',
['Transgenic systems can produce biological products','Rosie is the familiar transgenic-cow example','Human alpha-lactalbumin was the milk-protein example','Product use and animal welfare require assessment'],
['Introduced product-related genetic information','Expression in a suitable tissue','Recover and characterize the biological product','Evaluate purpose, quality and welfare'],
`Transgenic animal systems can be used to study or produce selected biological molecules, including proteins expressed in milk in suitable examples. Rosie is the familiar historical transgenic cow comparison associated with milk containing human alpha lactalbumin. The example illustrates expression of a particular human protein, not transformation of cow milk into an identical copy of human milk in every respect. A production observation also does not by itself prove that a preparation is suitable for every infant or medical use. Quality, composition and evidence for the intended purpose remain separate questions. Transgenic models can additionally contribute to vaccine safety research and chemical safety testing in defined contexts, but one animal result does not replace all later evaluation. Distinguish the animal, the introduced gene, the expressed product and the tested outcome. Ethical oversight, welfare and an appropriate comparison are part of evaluating the research, alongside the molecular fact that a specified biological product can be expressed.`,
[q('The familiar Rosie example concerns milk containing human:','Alpha-lactalbumin',['C-peptide as the only milk protein','Cry1Ab as the definition of milk','Agarose'],'The example concerns one expressed protein, not identity with human milk in every respect.'),q('Detecting a product in transgenic milk alone establishes universal clinical suitability. This is:','Incorrect; purpose-specific quality and outcome evidence remain necessary',['Always correct','The definition of a transgene','The definition of an antigen'],'Expression and suitability for use are separate conclusions.')]);
c('Biosafety: hazard, exposure and the GEAC distinction',
['Risk assessment considers hazard and exposure','Food and environmental endpoints differ','Gene flow and non-target effects need context','GEAC now expands to Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee'],
['Specific organism, product and intended use','Potential hazard + exposure pathways','Relevant evidence and safeguards','Appraisal and monitoring under the applicable system'],
`Biosafety evaluates possible effects of a biological organism, product or activity in its intended context. A potential hazard and actual exposure are related but distinct parts of risk reasoning. Food composition, allergenicity, non target effects, persistence and gene flow can raise different assessment questions. No single observation establishes that all genetically modified products are either universally safe or universally harmful. Assessment concerns the actual trait, organism, environment and use. In India, GEAC currently expands to Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee, within the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change. Older textbook wording may say Approval Committee; the official name changed in twenty ten. The committee has an environmental appraisal role, including relevant release proposals, rather than serving as a synonym for every medical regulator. This foundation explains the biological assessment categories and the current name, without claiming that one classroom example establishes legal permission for any particular activity. Monitoring and evidence remain important after an initial comparison.`,
[q('The current official expansion of GEAC is:','Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee',['Genetic Engineering Approval Council','General Enzyme Amplification Committee','Genetic Ecology Antibody Commission'],'The older Approval wording should be distinguished from the current official name.'),q('A sound biosafety comparison evaluates:','The specific trait, use, exposure and relevant endpoints',['Only the label GM','Only whether a gene has a long name','Only one universal claim about every organism'],'Risk reasoning depends on the actual biological context.')]);
c('Biopatents, biopiracy and traditional knowledge',
['A patent concerns defined claims, not every resource','Prior knowledge matters when assessing novelty','Biopiracy concerns unauthorized or inequitable exploitation','Benefit sharing differs from sequence ownership'],
['Biological resource / associated knowledge','Defined use or proposed invention','Prior knowledge, consent and benefit questions','Assess the actual claim and applicable framework'],
`Biopatent discussions concern claimed inventions involving biological material or processes. A patent is not a universal statement that someone owns every organism sharing a common name. Its scope depends on the actual claims and the applicable legal framework. Novelty and prior knowledge matter, so a well established traditional use cannot simply be treated as newly discovered because it is described by a different institution. Biopiracy is the broad examination concept of unauthorized or inequitable exploitation of biological resources and associated traditional knowledge without appropriate recognition or benefit arrangements. The familiar Basmati discussion illustrates why long established varieties and community knowledge complicate sweeping novelty claims. It should not be shortened into the false assertion that every Basmati grain everywhere became privately owned. Consent, access, recognition and benefit sharing address related but different concerns. This is a conceptual Biology comparison rather than advice about filing or challenging a patent. Read the claimed invention and the evidence of prior knowledge before drawing a conclusion.`,
[q('Why is prior traditional knowledge relevant to a biopatent discussion?','It can bear on whether a claimed invention is genuinely novel',['It automatically proves every patent valid','It changes every protein into DNA','It eliminates all benefit-sharing questions'],'A claim must be assessed against what was already known.'),q('Biopiracy chiefly concerns:','Unauthorized or inequitable exploitation of biological resources and associated knowledge',['Every ordinary act of cell division','Only an ELISA enzyme signal','Only the removal of insulin C-peptide'],'Resource access, recognition and benefits are distinct from molecular mechanism.')]);
c('Integrated biotechnology reasoning and remaining practice',
['Identify gene, vector, host, product and target','Separate DNA copying from protein expression','Match each claim with its actual measurement','Further vector-map and mixed-case practice remains'],
['Which biological object is named?','Which molecular action occurs?','What observation was actually measured?','What conclusion and limitation follow?'],
`Finish this unit by connecting objects, actions and evidence. A cry gene is DNA, a Cry protein is an encoded product, a crop is the expression context and a susceptible insect is a biological target. An insulin coding sequence is not mature insulin, and a white reporter colony is not full proof of an intended insert. PCR copy arithmetic describes an ideal model; ELISA reports a specified binding interaction. A gene therapy cell strategy has a persistence question, while a transgenic animal model has a relevance question. Biosafety and benefit sharing add assessment categories that molecular success alone cannot settle. When a mixed question appears, name the object first, trace the action second and identify the measurement third. Then choose the conclusion supported by that evidence. This recording provides the Unit nine foundation rather than exhaustive mastery. Further labelled vector maps, detailed experimental controls, multi step assay interpretation and mixed biotechnology application cases remain. The next planned Biology unit is Ecology and Environment.`,
[q('Which sequence of reasoning best handles a mixed biotechnology case?','Identify the object, mechanism, measurement and justified conclusion',['Assume every positive signal proves every claim','Treat a gene and its protein as identical','Ignore the host and target'],'Category separation prevents mechanism and evidence errors.'),q('A correct-sized DNA band alone proves a finished medicine is effective. This is:','Incorrect; identity, activity, quality and outcome evidence differ',['Always correct','The definition of purification','The definition of immunity'],'A size observation supports a narrower conclusion.')]);
const quiz=raw.map(([question,correct,wrong,explanation],i)=>{const answer=i%4,options=[...wrong];options.splice(answer,0,correct);return{question,options,answer,explanation}});
